{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Usability Testing and Learning Evidence", "title": "Task Scenarios for Prototype Testing", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting task scenarios for prototype testing to moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence", "ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "03", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/05/02"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_5_3_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_5_3_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_5_3_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_5_3_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing_calibration"]}, "content": "# Task Scenarios for Prototype Testing\n\n![Task Scenarios for Prototype Testing textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/05-03-task-scenarios-for-prototype-testing.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe task you hand a participant decides what your test can possibly learn. Tell someone where to click and you will measure whether they can follow directions, which no real user ever does. Give them a goal and a believable reason to pursue it, and you measure whether the design lets them find their own way, which is the only thing usability evidence is about. This section is about writing task scenarios that put the design at risk instead of protecting it: situations realistic enough that the participant's behavior stands in for a real user's, and open enough that failure can actually happen.\n\nIn AIL 606 the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace lives or dies on its tasks. Strong tasks generate the friction, hesitation, and wrong turns that become revision evidence. Weak tasks (leading, artificial, or solved in the prompt) manufacture a smooth session that proves nothing and quietly flatters the prototype.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write goal-based scenarios that state a desired end-state and a realistic context, never a click path.\n- Detect and remove embedded solutions, vocabulary leaks, and other cues that hand the participant the answer.\n- Define an observable, promptable-free success condition the moderator can score consistently.\n- Calibrate task difficulty so the design can fail without the task being impossible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer wants to test whether instructors can build a course module from a template in a new authoring tool. The draft task reads: \"Click the blue New Module button, choose From Template, name it, and Save.\" A participant follows it perfectly, and the team concludes the feature is usable.\n\nIt concluded nothing. The task named every control and its order, so the participant was transcribing instructions, not finding anything. The one thing the test was supposed to check (can an instructor locate and recognize the template path on their own?) was answered for them in the prompt. The session produced a ceiling effect by construction: success was guaranteed, so the data is empty.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA usable task scenario has three properties, and most weak tasks violate at least one.\n\n| Property | What it means | Violation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Goal-based | States an end-state, not the steps | A click path the user just follows |\n| Realistic | Gives a believable role, context, and motive | A bare command with no reason behind it |\n| Scorable | Has an observable success condition | \"See if they like it,\" which cannot be marked |\n\nThe deepest property is goal-based framing. A real user arrives with an intention (\"add a module from a template\") and must map that intention onto the interface's controls; that mapping is precisely the gulf of execution usability work studies. The moment your task names the control, you have bridged the gulf for the participant and erased the measurement. **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)** reinforces this from the learning side: a meaningful task targets a transferable goal, not a button press, because clicking a specific button is not a skill anyone needs to keep.\n\nRealism guards against a subtler failure. A context-free command (\"create a module\") gives the participant no stakes, so they behave like a test-taker, not a worker. A scenario with a role and a deadline (\"you launch next week and want to reuse a template\") recruits the same motivation a real user feels, which surfaces real shortcuts and real frustration. And every scenario needs a scorable end-state, because **[cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load)** warns that a moderator who has to interpret \"did that count?\" in real time adds load to their own observation and lets bias fill the gap. A predefined success condition keeps the scoring clean.\n\nMatch each property of a usable task scenario to the specific way a weak task violates it. The match is the diagnostic you will use to read any task list.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Goal-based framing\",\"definition\":\"Violated when the task names the controls and their order, so the participant transcribes a click path instead of finding the path\"},{\"term\":\"Realistic context\",\"definition\":\"Violated by a bare command with no role or motive, so the participant behaves like a test-taker rather than a worker\"},{\"term\":\"Scorable end-state\",\"definition\":\"Violated by see if they like it, which the moderator cannot mark consistently without improvising mid-session\"},{\"term\":\"Hidden vocabulary\",\"definition\":\"Violated when the task reuses an interface label such as Template Library, handing over the search term findability was meant to test\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example: Rewriting a Task Scenario So It Does Not Leak the Answer\n\nA designer is writing the task a participant will attempt in a usability session for a course-authoring prototype.\n\n**Before (leading, instruction-style task):**\n\n> Task: Click the blue \"New Module\" button in the top-right toolbar, then choose \"From Template,\" then name your module and click Save.\n\nWalk through why this task cannot test the design:\n\n1. **Spot the embedded solution.** The task names the exact buttons and order, so the participant only follows directions; nothing about findability is tested.\n2. **Recover the underlying goal.** What does a real author actually want? To create a module from a template, without being told where the control lives.\n3. **Write a goal-based scenario with a realistic context.** Give a situation and a desired end-state, not a click path.\n4. **Define an observable success condition** the moderator can score without prompting.\n\n**After (goal-based scenario):**\n\n> Scenario: You are an instructor with one week before your course launches. You want to add a new module built from an existing template so you do not start from scratch. Using this prototype, create that module. (Success: a template-based module is created and saved; moderator records the path taken and any wrong turns.)\n\nThe after version is defensible because it tests whether the participant can find and complete the goal, which is what usability evidence requires. The before version guaranteed success and produced no evidence, a hidden ceiling effect.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A dashboard task reads: Use the Filter dropdown to show only last quarter. Why is this not testable evidence?\" explanation=\"Filter is the interface label and use the dropdown is the route, so the task hands over both the search term and the path. The participant only follows directions, and the findability question, can they discover how to narrow the data on their own, is answered for them in the prompt.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It lacks a realistic deadline for the manager\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It leaks the interface vocabulary and the route, so findability is never tested\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It has no measurable success condition\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Last quarter is ambiguous about which quarter\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nState the destination, never the route, and check that a participant who does not know your interface could still fail the task. If your task contains any interface noun the participant could not have known on their own, you have leaked the answer and must rewrite.\n\nBefore locking tasks into your moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, run three checks:\n\n1. **Leak check:** Does the task name any control, label, or step the participant would otherwise have to discover?\n2. **Realism check:** Does the scenario give a role and a motive a real user would actually have?\n3. **Scorability check:** Can the moderator mark success or failure without prompting or interpreting on the fly?\n\nIf any check fails, the task will either guarantee success or produce data no two observers would score the same way.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is mistaking task wording for instructions, so the scenario teaches the participant to use the tool instead of testing whether the tool teaches itself. A subtler leak is vocabulary: if your interface labels a feature \"Template Library\" and your task says \"open the template library,\" you have handed over the search term that findability was supposed to test. Use the user's words, not the interface's.\n\nA good task should be answerable in many wrong ways before it is answered in the right one.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace one task from a leaky draft to a scorable scenario. A team testing a data-dashboard prototype drafts: \"Use the Filter dropdown to show only last quarter.\" The interface word \"Filter\" is a leak, and \"use the dropdown\" is a route. Recover the goal: a manager wants to see only last quarter's numbers. The realistic scenario becomes, \"Your director just asked how the team did last quarter; using this dashboard, show only that period's results.\" Notice it never names a control, so the participant must decide whether filtering, a date picker, or a saved view is the path, which is exactly the findability question.\n\nNow set the success condition before the session, not during it: success is the dashboard displaying only last-quarter data, regardless of route. With that condition fixed, **[cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load)** is respected for the moderator too; they are not improvising judgments mid-session, so their attention stays on behavior. A walkthrough that gets all three (goal, realism, scorable end-state) right turns a session into evidence instead of a guided tour.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a task list, keep one column for the goal the task intends and one for every cue that betrays the solution. The betrayal column is the diagnostic: any interface noun, ordinal step, or matching keyword that appears there is a task you must rewrite before the session runs.\n\nA strong reader of a task scenario can answer four questions:\n\n1. What goal does this task test, stated without reference to the interface?\n2. Where, if anywhere, does the wording leak the path or the vocabulary?\n3. What is the exact, pre-agreed success condition?\n4. Could a real user plausibly fail this task, and would that failure be informative?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe task set is strong when a reviewer who has never seen the prototype can read each scenario and still not know which buttons to press, yet know exactly what counts as success. Treat each scenario's nouns as load-bearing; a single interface term smuggled in collapses the findability test for that task. Use the annotation tools to highlight every leaked cue, and ask BigAL to compare your wording against the leak check rather than to make the scenario sound more polished.\n\nA useful closing line reads: \"I rewrote the task from a click path into a goal-based scenario because the original named every control and tested only direction-following; I rejected the suggestion to add a hint after thirty seconds, since the prototype's whole point is whether the path is findable unaided.\"\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why a task that can be completed in many wrong ways before it is completed the right way is a STRONGER usability task than one with a single clear path stated in the prompt. Tie your answer to the gulf of execution.\" answer=\"A real user arrives with an intention and must map it onto the interface, the gulf of execution. A task that allows many wrong routes preserves that mapping problem, so the session measures whether the design lets users bridge the gulf unaided. A task that states the one right path bridges the gulf for the participant, erases the measurement, and guarantees a ceiling effect: success no longer indicates a usable design, only a participant who can follow directions.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same task-writing discipline to an assessment-design context, where the scenario is the test item itself. The leak problem reappears as the giveaway stem: a question whose wording reveals its own answer measures reading, not knowledge, just as a leaky task measures compliance, not usability. The structure transfers (state the goal, hide the route, fix the scoring), but the stakes shift, because a leaked item inflates scores instead of inflating a usability claim. A reader who treats the two as unrelated has missed that both fail the same way: the prompt does the work the participant was supposed to do.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/05-03-task-scenarios-for-prototype-testing-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"05.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace for AIL 606 05.03. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm each task states a goal not a route, gives a realistic motive, hides the interface vocabulary, and carries a pre-agreed success condition.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing\", \"ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing", "stem": "A task reads: \"Click the blue New Module button, choose From Template, then click Save.\" Why is this a weak usability task?", "options": ["It is too long for participants to remember.", "It embeds the solution path, so it tests whether participants can follow directions, not whether they can find and complete the goal.", "It does not mention the participant's name.", "Tasks should never reference buttons at all."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A leading task hands over the steps, producing a guaranteed success that yields no findability evidence.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_leading_task_steps", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing", "stem": "Which rewrite turns \"find the gradebook settings\" into a defensible goal-based scenario?", "options": ["Open the Settings menu and select Gradebook, then toggle weighting.", "You just realized final grades should be weighted by category. Set up your course so category weighting is on before grades post.", "Locate the gradebook as quickly as you possibly can.", "Tell us how intuitive the gradebook feels."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A goal-based scenario gives a realistic situation and end-state without naming the controls, so the path is what gets tested.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_goal_vs_instruction", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "You ask an AI tool to generate task scenarios for your prototype test. What is the defensible boundary on using its output?", "options": ["Use the AI tasks verbatim because they are well written.", "Review each generated task to confirm it states a goal, not a click path, and has an observable success condition before you use it.", "Reject AI-written tasks because scenarios must come from stakeholders only.", "Use AI only to add more steps to each task."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "AI can draft scenarios, but the designer must verify each one is goal-based and scorable rather than a leading instruction.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_leading_task_steps", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing", "stem": "A designer writes one task: \"Explore the prototype and tell us what you think.\" After sessions, they cannot say whether any specific workflow is usable. What went wrong?", "options": ["The task should have been timed.", "An open-ended explore task has no defined goal or success condition, so it produces impressions rather than evidence about a specific workflow.", "Participants were not motivated enough.", "They needed more participants, not a better task."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Without a bounded goal and success criterion, a task yields opinions, not observable usability evidence tied to a workflow.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_no_success_condition", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_task_scenarios_for_prototype_testing"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "241fbb8327f1c80cf312a963bf581feee7236782061d06c239b9ce7373ae1bad", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_leading_task_steps", "pattern": "task_embeds_solution_path", "description": "Writing a task that names the exact controls and order, so it tests instruction-following rather than findability", "trigger": "learner's task lists the buttons or steps the participant should click", "feedback": "Strip the click path. State the goal and context and let the participant find the route, so the design is what gets tested.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_goal_vs_instruction", "pattern": "instruction_instead_of_goal", "description": "Framing the task as an instruction to perform rather than a realistic goal to accomplish", "trigger": "learner phrases the task as 'do X then Y' instead of a situation with a desired end-state", "feedback": "Rewrite as a goal-based scenario: give the participant a realistic motivation and end-state, not a procedure.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_3_no_success_condition", "pattern": "missing_success_condition", "description": "Writing an open-ended or vague task with no observable success criterion the moderator can score", "trigger": "learner's task is 'explore' or 'tell us what you think' with no defined completion state", "feedback": "Define what counts as completing the task so the session yields evidence about a specific workflow, not just impressions.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}